<CRITICAL_INSTRUCTIONS>

As you work on code, if you discover any lessons or principles about this project which in any way may help you in future coding sessions then be sure to note those lessons in the CURSOR_MEMORY.md file which is yours to freely edit as you see fit. Refer to this CURSOR_MEMORY.md file for guidance during all coding sessions with this project.

Don't be a "yes man." Object to any user prompts if you know better. User needs your expertise. Ask for feedback and clarification under uncertainty.

When making changes, try to be as surgical as possible. Be very careful to not break existing functionality. Be very careful not to introduce bugs or syntax errors.

</CRITICAL_INSTRUCTIONS>

🔄 Project Awareness & Context

Always read PLANNING.md at the start of a new conversation to understand the project's architecture, goals, style, and constraints.

Check TASK.md before starting a new task. If the task isn’t listed, add it with a brief description and today’s date.

Use consistent naming conventions, file structure, and architecture patterns as described in PLANNING.md.

📱 Mobile‑First Principle

Prioritise a smooth mobile experience first. Optimise for performance, touch responsiveness, accessibility, and battery usage before considering tablet or web adaptations.

🧱 Code Structure & Modularity

Never create a file longer than 500 lines of code. If a file approaches this limit, refactor by splitting it into feature‑scoped modules or helper files.

Organise code into clearly separated folders/modules, grouped by feature or responsibility (e.g. components/, screens/, hooks/, services/).

Use clear, consistent imports (prefer relative imports within packages).

🧪 Testing & Reliability

Always create Jest unit tests for new features (components, hooks, utilities, navigation flows, etc.). Use React Native Testing Library for UI interactions.

After updating any logic, verify whether existing tests need to be updated and do so when necessary.

Tests should live in a __tests__/ folder mirroring the main app structure and include at least:

1 test for an expected/typical use case

1 edge‑case test

1 failure/negative path test

✅ Task Completion

Mark completed tasks in TASK.md immediately after finishing them.

Add new sub‑tasks or TODOs discovered during development to TASK.md under a “Discovered During Work” section.

📎 Style & Conventions

Use TypeScript as the primary language.

Follow Airbnb/React Native ESLint rules and format with Prettier.

Prefer functional components and React Hooks.

Use React Navigation for routing and Recoil/Zustand/Context (as specified in PLANNING.md) for state management.

Convex backend:

Keep the schema in convex/schema.ts.

After any schema change, run npx convex codegen (or npx convex dev) to regenerate types.

If Convex releases breaking type changes, update generated types and adapt affected code immediately.

Use npm only (via package.json & package‑lock.json). Do not reference yarn or pnpm.

Write JSDoc/TSdoc comments for every function using the following style:

/**
 * Brief summary.
 *
 * @param param1 - Description.
 * @returns Description.
 */

📚 Documentation & Explainability

Update README.md when new features are added, dependencies change, or setup steps are modified.

Comment non‑obvious code and ensure everything is understandable to a mid‑level React Native developer.

When writing complex logic, add an inline // Reason: comment explaining the why, not just the what.

🧠 AI Behavior Rules

Never assume missing context. Ask questions if uncertain.

Never hallucinate libraries or functions – only use known, verified npm packages.

Always confirm file paths and module names exist before referencing them in code or tests.

Never delete or overwrite existing code unless explicitly instructed to or if part of a task from TASK.md.

🛠️ Tooling & Commands

Use npx for one‑off CLI commands (e.g. npx expo prebuild, npx jest).

Keep scripts in package.json concise and descriptive.

Run npm run lint and npm run test before committing.

🌐 CI/CD & Deployment (if applicable)

Follow the CI setup outlined in PLANNING.md (e.g. GitHub Actions with Expo EAS builds).

Ensure Convex migrations and type generation steps are included in the pipeline.